Live-client recovery checkpoint (2026-10-11): Mesa d99455c / library 42352938,
kernel d229757e9e / b64c72e2. Native client status retains first loss even when
the global fault clears after reset. The reset query reports RESET and, until
successful recovery, RESET_IN_PROGRESS. Old devices/contexts stay lost and new
contexts on those files fail; fresh rendering requires a newly opened native
file. The Haiku winsys queries idle contexts too and uses native completion
instead of a legacy Linux-version no-op submission. It does not initiate resets.

The explicit native_queue_probe --live-recovery test keeps a faulting device
and an independent idle device open through an owned read-only permission fault
and one root-requested reset. The idle device first queries status after the
reset and correctly discovers its earlier loss. Prior successful and failed
context/CPU/sync fences retain their results; every idle BO word survives;
fresh Mesa rendering and cleanup succeed. Two post-reset hardware jobs retire
with no errors and no live resources/quarantine. This is native ac_drm API
qualification; application-level robust EGL/GL context loss remains untested.

