Robust EGL application / automatic recovery checkpoint (2026-10-11): fixture
0ddd796, automatic extension 8cdb918 / binary 4b62013f; kernel e8425eecbd /
f2c890a4. The installed Mesa library remains 42352938. Build the standalone
native_robustness_probe.cpp with private EGL/GLESv2 headers, this directory on
the include path, -std=c++17, and -lEGL -lGLESv2 -lbe. run-robustness.sh modes:
--software, --expect-no-device, --baseline, --manual and --auto. Hardware modes
require explicit native selection. --manual disables automatic policy only
while healthy, injects one owned permission fault, checks pending GL loss,
requests one live reset and restores initial policy after fresh rendering.
--auto requires automatic recovery already enabled and never issues a reset
ioctl. Both modes retain the robust EGL/GLES context across the independent
fault, require UNKNOWN_CONTEXT_RESET then completion, ensure old GL calls
return CONTEXT_LOST without writing pixels or submitting jobs, and destroy/
recreate the display/context to render 4,096 exact shader pixels again.

GLES robustness uses EGL_EXT_create_context_robustness attributes. An initial
fixture incorrectly used desktop-GL KHR flags and failed context creation
before any injected fault; preserved evidence records the correction. Manual
queued/synchronous trials pass on b64c72e2. Automatic queued/synchronous and
manual-policy trials pass on f2c890a4, as do kernel mixed queued faults, CP
wait timeout, and a non-terminating shader. Automatic recovery is enabled by
default, once per terminal fault after accepted work drains. Policy changes
cannot arm an existing fault, and old client/context loss remains permanent.
Live video and SDMA/UVD faults still exclude this GFX-only policy.

